WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.317 --> 00:00:07.618
Bonjour! My name is Sophie, and I live in France.

2
00:00:08.338 --> 00:00:13.480
Today I am visiting my grandfather at his vineyard where he grows grapes for making wine.

3
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.061
Wine is made from grapes and contains alcohol.

4
00:00:17.781 --> 00:00:19.941
I can't drink wine because I'm too young.

5
00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:24.363
I can tell you how it is made because my grandfather makes wine!

6
00:00:25.243 --> 00:00:31.285
Today adults drink wine with dinner at home or in a restaurant, but wine has a long history.

7
00:00:32.146 --> 00:00:36.889
Archaeologists discovered wine jugs in Iran that were seven thousand years old.

8
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:43.074
In Ancient Egypt the pharaohs were buried with wine so they could drink wine in the afterlife.

9
00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:50.399
The ancient Greeks and Romans drank wine mixed with water and used wine in religious ceremonies.

10
00:00:51.479 --> 00:00:57.724
When the Roman Empire expanded, the Romans brought wine grapes to France, Italy, Spain, and Germany.

11
00:00:58.956 --> 00:01:04.461
From the fifth century until the Middle Ages, Christian monks were the main winemakers of Europe.

12
00:01:05.261 --> 00:01:08.164
The monks made wine at the monasteries where they lived.

13
00:01:08.824 --> 00:01:13.027
They improved the quality of wine and developed better methods for making wine.

14
00:01:13.888 --> 00:01:17.291
Making wine is not difficult, but you need lots of patience.

15
00:01:17.931 --> 00:01:20.493
It takes at least a year before the wine is ready!

16
00:01:21.154 --> 00:01:23.576
Let me tell you how my grandfather makes wine.

17
00:01:24.236 --> 00:01:28.340
First Grandfather grows the grapes in cool weather with lots of sun.

18
00:01:29.384 --> 00:01:32.965
In France where we live, the weather is perfect for growing grapes.

19
00:01:33.985 --> 00:01:38.087
Grapes grow on vines, and the field where the grapes grow is called a vineyard.

20
00:01:39.607 --> 00:01:43.408
When the grapes are ripe, Grandfather harvests the grapes from the vines.

21
00:01:44.208 --> 00:01:47.669
He takes them to the winery and puts them in a huge vat.

22
00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:51.750
The ripe grapes are juicy and sweet and full of sugar.

23
00:01:52.551 --> 00:01:54.611
The next step is to crush the grapes.

24
00:01:55.812 --> 00:01:59.469
A long time ago, people used their feet to crush the grapes.

25
00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:05.553
They would climb into the vat with bare feet and jump and stomp until the grapes were crushed.

26
00:02:06.313 --> 00:02:09.635
Boy, that sounds like fun, and very messy too!

27
00:02:10.535 --> 00:02:13.457
My grandfather uses a machine to crush the grapes.

28
00:02:14.637 --> 00:02:19.260
Crushing the grapes breaks the skins and turns them into a wet, juicy pulp.

29
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:24.823
Over the next few days, yeast on the grape skins causes the wine to ferment.

30
00:02:25.543 --> 00:02:29.506
When the wine ferments, the sugar in the grapes changes to alcohol.

31
00:02:30.146 --> 00:02:32.908
The juice and pulp in the vat will begin to fizz.

32
00:02:34.309 --> 00:02:36.551
After a week the fizzing subsides.

33
00:02:37.331 --> 00:02:42.094
Now my grandfather must separate the liquid from the skins, pulp, and seeds.

34
00:02:42.875 --> 00:02:47.618
He uses a machine called a winepress, and then he pours the wine into barrels.

35
00:02:48.499 --> 00:02:51.541
Until now, the wine has been open to the air.

36
00:02:52.261 --> 00:02:55.644
But now the barrels are sealed to keep the air away from the wine.

37
00:02:57.164 --> 00:03:00.706
Three weeks later the fermentation is finally complete.

38
00:03:01.486 --> 00:03:03.847
Now the wine is ready for the final step.

39
00:03:04.727 --> 00:03:06.368
This step is called racking.

40
00:03:07.048 --> 00:03:13.471
My grandfather filters the leftover yeast, grape seeds, and grape skins from the wine, and scrapes the barrel

41
00:03:13.531 --> 00:03:18.633
clean. Then he pours the wine back into the barrel and seals it airtight.

42
00:03:20.314 --> 00:03:23.175
The barrels of wine are stored in a cool, dark place.

43
00:03:23.975 --> 00:03:27.633
Four months later, Grandfather racks the wine one more time.

44
00:03:28.454 --> 00:03:30.295
Now the wine is perfectly clear.

45
00:03:31.095 --> 00:03:36.438
Grandfather puts the barrels away in a cool, dark place again and leaves them for a whole year!

46
00:03:37.339 --> 00:03:40.180
My grandfather keeps his barrels of wine in the cellar.

47
00:03:41.861 --> 00:03:43.782
One year later, the wine is ready!

48
00:03:44.663 --> 00:03:50.426
Sometimes vineyards wait two years or longer before they sell their wine, and some wines are kept for

49
00:03:50.486 --> 00:03:54.388
decades. People say that wine gets better with age.

50
00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.505
The wine is poured into bottles, and each bottle is plugged with a cork to prevent air getting

51
00:04:00.625 --> 00:04:06.449
in. Corks are made from the bark of the cork tree, which grows mainly in Portugal.

52
00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.753
Cork is very elastic, but it is also airtight.

53
00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:13.075
It is ideal for wine bottles.

54
00:04:13.796 --> 00:04:18.880
France is famous for its wine, and many tourists visit France just to tour the wineries.

55
00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.624
However, wine grapes are grown in warm climates all over the world.

56
00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:32.005
France and Italy have the oldest wineries, but Australia, New Zealand, Chile, South Africa, and California are all

57
00:04:32.086 --> 00:04:33.647
famous for their wine as well.

58
00:04:34.687 --> 00:04:37.870
Now I will help Grandfather get the wine ready for the stores.

59
00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:41.512
First, we put fancy labels on the bottles.

60
00:04:41.833 --> 00:04:44.254
Then, we put the wine bottles into boxes.

61
00:04:45.015 --> 00:04:52.060
Tomorrow a truck will pick up the boxes and take them to restaurants, bars, supermarkets, and wine stores.

62
00:04:52.896 --> 00:04:55.262
Thank you for visiting my grandfather's vineyard.

63
00:04:55.984 --> 00:04:58.390
Now you know everything about making wine.

64
00:04:59.172 --> 00:05:01.919
Next time you can help us harvest the grapes!
